# Linux Zero to Hero — Sections 7, 8, 9 & 10: Process Management, Monitoring, Networking & Disk Management

## Section 7 — Process Management

### What is a Process?

A **process** is a running instance of any program. Examples:
- A Python application executing on a server
- A running shell script
- A web server (Apache, Nginx) actively serving requests
- A background cron job

Programs cannot directly interact with hardware (CPU, memory, disk). The **operating system** manages hardware allocation. However, if one process consumes excessive CPU or memory, it starves other processes of resources.

---

### Why Process Management Matters

**Scenario:** A server has one CPU running three programs — a Python ML model (CPU-intensive), a web server, and a cron job.

- The Python ML model consumes **90% CPU** → web server gets only 10% → cron job gets nothing.
- Similarly, a Java application with many threads might consume **90% memory** → web server throws **Out of Memory** errors.

As a Linux administrator or DevOps engineer, the three core process management activities are:

1. **View / check** running processes
2. **Kill, stop, or resume** processes
3. **Prioritize or deprioritize** processes

---

### Viewing Running Processes

```bash
# List processes for the current session
ps

# List ALL running processes with details
ps aux

# Count total number of running processes
ps aux | nl          # shows line numbers
ps aux | wc -l       # shows total count

# Alternative listing (without CPU/memory columns)
ps -ef

# Count using ps -ef
ps -ef | wc -l
```

#### Understanding `ps aux` Output Columns

| Column | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `USER` | The user who started the process |
| `PID` | Unique **Process ID** — used to manage the process |
| `%CPU` | CPU utilization percentage |
| `%MEM` | Memory utilization percentage |
| `TTY` | Terminal associated with the process |
| `START` | Time the process started |
| `COMMAND` | The command that launched the process |

#### `ps aux` vs `ps -ef`

| Feature | `ps aux` | `ps -ef` |
|---|---|---|
| CPU utilization | ✅ Yes | ✅ Yes |
| Memory utilization | ✅ Yes | ❌ No |
| Use case | Detailed resource monitoring | General process listing |

> Use `ps aux` when you need both CPU **and** memory utilization data.

#### Filtering for a Specific Process

```bash
# Find a Java process
ps aux | grep java

# Exclude the grep command itself from results
ps aux | grep java | grep -v grep
```

---

### Killing, Stopping & Resuming Processes

#### Kill a Process

```bash
# Kill a process by PID (graceful termination)
kill <PID>

# Force kill a process (when regular kill doesn't work)
kill -9 <PID>

# Get thread dumps from a Java process (useful for debugging)
kill -3 <PID>
```

**`kill` vs `kill -9`:**
- `kill <PID>` sends a termination signal — the process can handle it gracefully.
- `kill -9 <PID>` forcefully terminates the process immediately — no cleanup. Use this when a process is completely unresponsive.

#### Stop and Resume a Process

```bash
# Temporarily stop (pause) a process
kill -STOP <PID>

# Resume a stopped process
kill -CONT <PID>
```

#### Practical Workflow: Kill a Problematic Process

```bash
# Step 1: Find the process and its PID
ps aux | grep java | grep -v grep

# Step 2: Kill it
kill <PID>

# Step 3: If it doesn't die, force kill
kill -9 <PID>
```

#### Background & Foreground Processes

```bash
# Run a command in the background
command &

# Bring a background process to foreground
fg

# List background jobs
jobs
```

---

### Prioritizing & Deprioritizing Processes

Linux uses a **niceness value** (ranging from **-20 to +19**) to control process priority at the CPU level:

- **Lower niceness value (e.g., -20)** → **Higher CPU priority**
- **Higher niceness value (e.g., +19)** → **Lower CPU priority**

> **Analogy:** A doctor prioritizes ICU patients. Similarly, the CPU prioritizes processes with lower niceness values. Use `renice` to override this and tell the CPU how important a process actually is.

```bash
# Deprioritize a process (reduce its CPU priority)
renice -n 10 -p <PID>

# Increase a process's CPU priority (use with caution)
renice -n -5 -p <PID>
```

> ⚠️ Use `renice` carefully. Only modify priorities if you fully understand what processes are running on the server.

---

### Services vs Processes

| Aspect | Process | Service |
|---|---|---|
| What it is | Any running program | A special background process |
| Starts automatically on boot | ❌ No | ✅ Yes |
| Examples | Python script, shell script | Apache, Nginx, cron, sshd |
| Management command | `kill`, `ps` | `systemctl` |

**Key distinction:** Services are designed to **survive server restarts**. When a server reboots, services automatically start back up. Regular processes do not.

> Most modern software installers (web servers, databases) automatically register themselves as services so they restart on boot without manual intervention.

---

### Managing Services with systemctl

```bash
# List all running services
systemctl list-units --type=service

# Check the status of a specific service
systemctl status cron

# Stop a service
systemctl stop cron

# Start a service
systemctl start cron

# Restart a service
systemctl restart cron

# Enable a service to start automatically on boot
systemctl enable cron

# Disable a service from starting on boot
systemctl disable cron
```

---

## Section 8 — System Monitoring

### Monitoring CPU, Memory & Disk

#### CPU & Process Monitoring

```bash
# Real-time process and resource monitor (interactive)
top

# Enhanced visual version of top (install if needed)
htop
```

Both `top` and `htop` display:
- Real-time CPU and memory utilization per process
- Total system uptime
- Number of running tasks

`htop` provides a more visually informative interface with color-coded graphs.

> **Limitation:** `top` and `htop` are interactive — they cannot be used inside shell scripts for automated monitoring.

```bash
# Check number of CPUs on the machine
nproc
```

#### Memory Monitoring

```bash
# Check memory usage (raw output — good for scripting)
free

# Check memory in megabytes
free -m

# Check memory in human-readable format (KB/MB/GB)
free -h

# Example output:
#               total    used    free   shared  buff/cache  available
# Mem:          557MB   360MB   220MB     ...
```

```bash
# Report virtual memory statistics (system performance overview)
vmstat
```

Use `free -h` for quick checks. Use `vmstat` when debugging slow or underperforming instances — it shows free memory and cache usage.

#### Disk Space Monitoring

```bash
# Check disk space usage across all partitions (human-readable)
df -h

# Check disk usage folder by folder
du -sh /opt/*

# Example: find large folders inside /opt
cd /opt
du -sh *
```

`df -h` shows overall partition utilization. `du -sh` drills into specific directories to find which folders consume the most disk space — useful when debugging "disk full" issues.

---

### Monitoring Commands Summary

| Resource | Quick Check | Scriptable | Real-time |
|---|---|---|---|
| **CPU** | `top`, `htop`, `nproc` | `nproc` | `top`, `htop` |
| **Memory** | `top`, `htop`, `free -h` | `free -h` | `top`, `htop` |
| **Disk Space** | `df -h` | `df -h` | — |
| **Disk by Folder** | `du -sh *` | `du -sh` | — |
| **All Processes** | `ps aux` | `ps aux` | `top` |

---

### Production Approach: Prometheus & Grafana

For production environments, CLI commands are used for quick, ad-hoc troubleshooting. For continuous monitoring with alerting, the standard approach is:

```
Linux Server
    ↓ (metrics scraped via Node Exporter)
Prometheus
    ↓ (data visualized)
Grafana Dashboards
    ↓ (alerts configured)
Email / Slack Notifications → DevOps / SRE Team
```

- **Node Exporter** runs on the Linux server and exposes CPU, memory, and disk metrics.
- **Prometheus** scrapes these metrics at regular intervals.
- **Grafana** visualizes the data and sends alerts when thresholds are breached.

> In interviews: mention both the CLI commands for quick troubleshooting **and** the Prometheus + Grafana setup for production-grade monitoring.

---

## Section 9 — Linux Networking

Linux networking is a foundational topic covering IP addresses, subnets, private/public networks, and the OSI model.

**Core networking concepts to study:**
- IP addressing and subnetting
- Private vs public subnets
- CIDR notation
- The OSI model (7 layers) with practical examples
- Cloud networking (AWS VPC, Azure VNet)

> This section references an external networking fundamentals series for in-depth coverage of these topics.

**Key Linux networking commands for quick reference:**

```bash
# Check network interfaces and IP addresses
ip addr show
# or (older systems)
ifconfig

# Check network connectivity
ping google.com

# Trace the route packets take to a destination
traceroute google.com

# Check open ports and active connections
netstat -tulnp
# or (modern replacement)
ss -tulnp

# DNS lookup
nslookup google.com
dig google.com

# Check if a specific port is reachable
telnet <host> <port>
curl -v <url>

# Display routing table
ip route show
```

---

## Section 10 — Disk Management

### Why Disk Management?

A Linux server starts with a fixed disk size (e.g., 8 GB). Over time:
- Applications generate log files continuously.
- More packages and software get installed.
- Companies archive/compress logs but eventually still run out of space.

When storage is exhausted, adding a new volume is the standard solution. The process involves:
1. Creating a new volume (e.g., in AWS EBS)
2. Attaching it to the instance
3. Formatting it to a usable file system (`ext4` or `xfs`)
4. Mounting it to a directory on the Linux file system

---

### Viewing Attached Volumes & Partitions

```bash
# List all block devices attached to the instance
lsblk

# Example output:
# NAME    MAJ:MIN RM  SIZE RO TYPE MOUNTPOINT
# xvda    202:0    0    8G  0 disk
# ├─xvda1 202:1    0    7G  0 part /
# └─xvda14 202:14  0    1G  0 part /boot
# xvdf    202:80   0   10G  0 disk        ← newly attached volume

# Get detailed partition information
sudo fdisk -l
```

> `lsblk` entries starting with `loop` are virtual devices used for snapshots/ISO images — ignore them. Focus on `xvda` (primary) and any newly attached volumes like `xvdf`.

**Understanding partitions:**
- A raw block storage volume (e.g., 8 GB EBS) is split into **partitions**.
- Each partition is formatted to a file system type (`ext4`, `xfs`) and mounted to a directory (`/`, `/boot`, etc.).
- `xvda1` → mounted at `/` (root, 7 GB)
- `xvda14` → mounted at `/boot` (1 GB, used during startup)

---

### Adding & Mounting a New Volume (AWS EC2 Example)

#### Step 1: Create and Attach the Volume in AWS

1. Go to **EC2 → Elastic Block Store → Volumes → Create Volume**.
2. Set size (e.g., 10 GB).
3. **Critical:** Select the **same Availability Zone** as the EC2 instance (e.g., `us-east-1c`). Volumes and instances must be in the same AZ to attach.
4. After creation, click **Actions → Attach Volume** and select the target instance.
5. Set device name (e.g., `/dev/sdf`).

#### Step 2: Verify the Volume is Visible

```bash
lsblk
# You should now see xvdf (10G) listed without a mount point
```

#### Step 3: Create a Mount Directory

```bash
mkdir -p /mnt/demo-volume
```

#### Step 4: Format the Volume

Block storage cannot be used directly by applications. It must be formatted to a file system type first.

```bash
# Format to ext4 file system
sudo mkfs -t ext4 /dev/xvdf

# Alternative: format to xfs
sudo mkfs -t xfs /dev/xvdf
```

| File System | Notes |
|---|---|
| `ext4` | Most widely used Linux file system — good default choice |
| `xfs` | High-performance, preferred for large files and high throughput |

#### Step 5: Mount the Volume

```bash
# Mount the formatted volume to the directory
sudo mount /dev/xvdf /mnt/demo-volume

# Verify the mount
lsblk
df -h
```

#### Step 6: Use the New Storage

```bash
cd /mnt/demo-volume
touch testfile
ls -ltr
# testfile is created on the new 10 GB volume
```

#### Making the Mount Persistent (Survives Reboots)

By default, mounts created with `mount` are lost on reboot. To make them permanent, add an entry to `/etc/fstab`:

```bash
# Get the UUID of the new volume
sudo blkid /dev/xvdf

# Add to /etc/fstab
echo "UUID=<your-uuid>  /mnt/demo-volume  ext4  defaults,nofail  0  2" | sudo tee -a /etc/fstab
```

#### Unmounting a Volume

```bash
sudo umount /mnt/demo-volume
```

---

### Disk Management Commands Summary

| Command | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `lsblk` | List all block devices and their mount points |
| `sudo fdisk -l` | Detailed partition information |
| `df -h` | Disk space usage by file system partition |
| `du -sh *` | Disk usage by folder (drill-down) |
| `mkfs -t ext4 /dev/xvdf` | Format a volume to ext4 file system |
| `mkfs -t xfs /dev/xvdf` | Format a volume to xfs file system |
| `mount /dev/xvdf /mnt/dir` | Mount a formatted volume to a directory |
| `umount /mnt/dir` | Unmount a volume |
| `blkid /dev/xvdf` | Show UUID and file system type of a volume |

---

## Interview Questions to Remember

**Q1: What is the difference between `ps aux` and `ps -ef`?**

Both list all running processes. `ps aux` includes **CPU and memory utilization** columns, making it better for resource monitoring. `ps -ef` shows a full process listing but does **not** include memory utilization. Use `ps aux` when diagnosing resource consumption.

---

**Q2: What is the difference between `kill` and `kill -9`?**

`kill <PID>` sends a `SIGTERM` signal — a graceful termination request that allows the process to clean up before exiting. `kill -9 <PID>` sends `SIGKILL` — an immediate, forceful termination that cannot be caught or ignored by the process. Use `kill -9` only when a process is unresponsive to a regular kill.

---

**Q3: What is the difference between a process and a service?**

A **process** is any running instance of a program. A **service** is a special type of background process that starts automatically when the server boots. Services are managed with `systemctl`. Web servers (Nginx, Apache) and system daemons (cron, sshd) are services. Regular application scripts are processes.

---

**Q4: How would you troubleshoot a Linux server that is running slow?**

1. Run `top` or `htop` to identify which process is consuming excessive CPU or memory.
2. Run `free -h` to check available memory — look for low free memory or excessive swap usage.
3. Run `df -h` to check if disk is full — a full disk causes severe slowdowns.
4. Run `du -sh *` in key directories to find large files consuming disk space.
5. Kill or renice the offending process if identified.
6. In production: check Prometheus/Grafana dashboards for historical trends and triggered alerts.

---

**Q5: Why must a new EBS volume be formatted before use?**

A newly created EBS volume is raw **block storage**. Operating systems and applications cannot read or write to raw block storage — they require a structured **file system** (like `ext4` or `xfs`). Formatting creates this structure. After formatting, the volume must also be **mounted** to a directory before it becomes accessible within the Linux file system.

---

## Full Commands Quick Reference

### Process Management

```bash
ps aux                          # List all processes with CPU/memory
ps -ef                          # List all processes (no memory column)
ps aux | wc -l                  # Count running processes
ps aux | grep java | grep -v grep  # Find a specific process
kill <PID>                      # Graceful kill
kill -9 <PID>                   # Force kill
kill -3 <PID>                   # Get Java thread dumps
kill -STOP <PID>                # Pause a process
kill -CONT <PID>                # Resume a paused process
renice -n 10 -p <PID>           # Lower CPU priority
renice -n -5 -p <PID>           # Raise CPU priority
command &                       # Run in background
jobs                            # List background jobs
fg                              # Bring background job to foreground
```

### Service Management

```bash
systemctl list-units --type=service   # List all services
systemctl status <service>             # Check service status
systemctl start <service>              # Start a service
systemctl stop <service>               # Stop a service
systemctl restart <service>            # Restart a service
systemctl enable <service>             # Auto-start on boot
systemctl disable <service>            # Disable auto-start
```

### System Monitoring

```bash
top                    # Real-time process and resource monitor
htop                   # Visual real-time monitor
nproc                  # Number of CPUs
free -h                # Memory usage (human-readable)
vmstat                 # Virtual memory and system performance stats
df -h                  # Disk space by partition
du -sh *               # Disk usage by folder
```

### Disk Management

```bash
lsblk                              # List block devices
sudo fdisk -l                      # Detailed partition info
mkfs -t ext4 /dev/xvdf             # Format volume to ext4
mkfs -t xfs /dev/xvdf              # Format volume to xfs
mkdir -p /mnt/demo-volume          # Create mount point
mount /dev/xvdf /mnt/demo-volume   # Mount the volume
umount /mnt/demo-volume            # Unmount the volume
blkid /dev/xvdf                    # Show UUID and FS type
df -h                              # Verify mount and space
```
